+++
title: Empire
description: Multiple states under one central authority, usually created by conquest
hook: Most people throughout history have lived under imperial rule. Many
  empires endured for centuries, and the age of the ancient Egyptian, Chinese
  and Japanese empires is counted in millennia.
standfirst: Empires can be traced as far back as recorded history goes, and
  were the dominant international organization until the 20th century at
  least. A century ago most of the world was ruled by people who called
  themselves Emperors; of the great powers, only the United States and France
  were republics.
key-facts:
  - name: In a sentence
    value: Multiple states under one central authority, usually created by conquest
  - name: The word comes from
    value: The Roman concept of //Imperium//
  - name: Whose head of state is
    value: An emperor
  - name: A kind of
    value: Realm; sovereign state
  - name: Studied by
    value: Imperial history
  - name: Not to be confused with
    value: imperium, Empire, Imperia
pull-quotes:
  - text: In previous times events in the world occurred without impinging on one
      another \... [Then] history became a whole, as if a single body; events
      in Italy and Libya came to be enmeshed with those in Asia and Greece,
      and everything gets directed towards one single goal.
    attribution: Polybius (2:3), the earliest thinker to approach universal empire
      theoretically
    section: universal-empire
  - text: the whole world celebrated a great bacchanal
    attribution: Sima Qian (d. 86 BC), 6:234, on Qin's conquest of 230–221 BC
    section: atomic-bomb-and-empire
  - text: words cannot express how bitterly we are hated
    attribution: Lucan, a classical Roman patriot, on the empire's subject peoples
    section: history
  - text: was neither holy, nor Roman, nor an empire
    attribution: Voltaire, on the Holy Roman Empire
    section: post-classical-period
  - text: For Russia to survive, it must remain an empire.
    attribution: Vladimir Putin
    section: russia
timeline:
  - when: around 3200 BC
    what: The earliest known empire appears in southern Egypt.
    from: The earliest known empire appeared in southern Egypt sometime around
      3200 BC.
  - when: 24th century BC
    what: Sargon of Akkad establishes the Akkadian Empire, an early
      all-Mesopotamian empire.
    from: The [[ Akkadian Empire ]], established by [[ Sargon of Akkad ]] (24th
      century BC), was an early all-Mesopotamian empire which spread into
      Anatolia, the Levant and Ancient Iran.
  - when: 1274 BC
    what: Egypt and the Hittites clash at the Battle of Kadesh.
    from: Egypt and the Hittites emerged as two dominant Empires of the club and
      in 1274 BC clashed in the [[ Battle of Kadesh ]].
  - when: 916–612 BC
    what: The Neo-Assyrian Empire, the first empire to recover from the late
      Bronze Age collapse.
    from: The first empire to recover from the collapse was the [[ Neo-Assyrian
      Empire ]] (916–612 BC).
  - when: 673 BC
    what: Assyria has conquered the entire Fertile Crescent, including Cyprus and
      Egypt.
    from: By 673 BC, Assyria conquered the entire [[ Fertile Crescent ]] including
      Cyprus and [[ Egypt | assyrian-conquest-of-egypt ]].
  - when: 612 BC
    what: Nineveh, the Assyrian capital, is razed by the combined armies of the
      Medes, Babylonians, Scythians and Cimmerians.
    from: In the 6th century BC, the [[ Median Empire ]], [[ Babylonians |
      neo-babylonian-empire ]], Scythians, and Cimmerians allied and [[
      defeated the Assyrian Empire |
      medo-babylonian-conquest-of-the-assyrian-empire ]]. The Assyrian
      capital, [[ Nineveh ]], was razed by their combined armies in 612 BC.
  - when: 550–330 BC
    what: The Achaemenid Empire, considered the first great empire in history or
      the first "world empire".
    from: The successful and extensive [[ Achaemenid Empire ]] (550–330 BC), also
      known as the first Persian Empire, covered [[ Mesopotamia ]], [[ Egypt
      ]], parts of [[ Greece ]], [[ Thrace ]], the [[ Middle East ]], much of
      [[ Central Asia ]], and North-Western [[ India ]]. It is considered the
      first great empire in history or the first "world empire".[[ ^77 ]]
  - when: 221 BC
    what: China becomes an empire as Qin ends the Warring States period.
    from: In 221 BC, China became an empire when the [[ State of Qin | qin-state
      ]] ended the chaotic [[ Warring States period ]] through its [[ conquest
      | qins-wars-of-unification ]] of the [[ other six states |
      seven-warring-states ]], starting the [[ Qin Empire | qin-dynasty ]]
      (221–207 BC).
  - when: AD 212
    what: Caracalla's edict extends Roman citizenship to all inhabitants of the
      Mediterranean world.
    from: Doyle exemplifies the transformation on the case of the Roman Emperor [[
      Caracalla ]] whose [[ edict in AD 212 | constitutio-antoniniana ]]
      extended the Roman citizenship to all inhabitants of the Mediterranean
      world.[[ ^416 ]]
  - when: 800 to 1806
    what: The //sacrum Romanum imperium//, the Holy Roman Empire.
    from: The //sacrum Romanum imperium// (Holy Roman Empire), which lasted from
      800 to 1806, claimed to have exclusively comprehended Christian
      principalities, and was only nominally a discrete imperial state.
  - when: "917"
    what: The Eastern Roman Empire is forced to recognize the Bulgarian ruler
      Simeon the Great's imperial title of Tsar.
    from: In [[ Southeastern | southeastern-europe ]] and [[ Eastern Europe ]],
      during 917, the [[ Eastern Roman Empire ]], sometimes called the
      Byzantine Empire, was forced to recognize the Imperial title of [[
      Bulgarian | bulgaria ]] ruler [[ Simeon the Great | simeon-i-of-bulgaria
      ]], who were then called [[ Tsar ]], the first ruler to hold that
      precise imperial title.
  - when: "1204"
    what: The Fourth Crusade takes Constantinople and the crusaders set up a Latin
      Empire there.
    from: "In 1204, after the [[ Fourth Crusade ]] conquered [[ Constantinople ]],
      the [[ crusaders | crusades ]] established a [[ Latin Empire ]]
      (1204–1261) in that city, while the defeated Byzantine Empire's
      descendants established two smaller, short-lived empires in [[ Asia
      Minor ]]: the [[ Empire of Nicaea ]] (1204–1261) and the [[ Empire of
      Trebizond ]] (1204–1461)."
  - when: "1547"
    what: Ivan the Terrible is crowned Emperor of Russia and begins the conquest
      of Siberia.
    from: In 1547, [[ Ivan the Terrible ]] was crowned [[ Emperor of Russia | tsar
      ]]. He transformed Russia from a medieval state to a fledgling empire
      and began the [[ conquest of Siberia | russian-conquest-of-siberia ]].
  - when: "1920"
    what: The British Empire is the largest empire in history.
    from: In 1920, the [[ British Empire ]] was [[ the largest empire in history |
      list-of-largest-empires ]].
  - when: 1989-1991
    what: The Soviet Empire collapses, leaving the United States the only
      superpower.
    from: The Soviet Empire collapsed in 1989-1991. The United States remained the
      only superpower, but whether its foreign policy qualifies as imperial is
      debatable (chapters "Contemporary usage" and 'Present" below).
image-plan:
  - file: Modern_Empires.svg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: The empires of the modern era, each drawn over the span of time it
      existed.
  - file: Portugal_Imério_total.png
    role: full
    section: colonial-empires
    caption: Every part of the world that was at some point Portuguese. In the
      early 16th century Portugal and the Spanish Empire together established
      the first global empire and trade network.
  - file: Modern_Empires_-_en.svg
    role: inline
    section: colonial-empires
    caption: The main empires of the modern era, 1492–1945.
  - file: Combined_Air_Operations_Center_151007-F-MS415-019.jpg
    role: inline
    section: present
    caption: Al Udeid Air Base in Qatar. Chalmers Johnson argues that the US
      global network of hundreds of military bases already represents a global
      empire in its initial form.
glossary:
  - term: metropole
    gloss: The center of an empire, which holds political control over the
      peripheries.
    from: The center of the empire (sometimes referred to as the [[ metropole ]])
      has political control over the peripheries.[[ ^2 ]]
  - term: federation
    gloss: An extensive state voluntarily composed of autonomous states and
      peoples — the thing an empire is contrasted with.
    from: This is in contrast to a [[ federation ]], which is an extensive state
      voluntarily composed of autonomous states and peoples.
  - term: Tellurocracy
    gloss: An empire relying on land forces and consisting mostly of contiguous
      territory, such as the Roman or Mongol empires.
    from: An important distinction has been between empires relying on land forces
      and consisting mostly of contiguous territory ([[ Tellurocracy ]]), such
      as the [[ Roman Empire ]] or the [[ Mongol Empire ]]; and those based on
      sea power ([[ Thalassocracy ]]) and which include territories that are
      remote from the core of the empire, such as the [[ Dutch Colonial Empire
      ]] or the [[ British Empire ]].[[ ^4 ]]
  - term: thalassocracy
    gloss: An empire composed of islands and coasts which are accessible to its
      terrestrial homeland.
    from: The terrestrial empire's maritime analogue is the [[ thalassocracy ]],
      an empire composed of islands and coasts which are accessible to its
      terrestrial homeland, such as the Athenian-dominated [[ Delian League ]]
      and the [[ British Empire ]]) with looser structures and more scattered
      territories, often consisting of many islands and other forms of
      possessions which required the creation and maintenance of a powerful
      navy.[[ ^42 ]]
  - term: imperium
    gloss: Latin, from //imperare//, "to command"; originally a magistrate's
      authority, usually in a military sense.
    from: The Latin word //[[ imperium ]]// derives from //imperare//, meaning "to
      command", and originally referred to a magistrate's authority (usually
      in a military sense).
  - term: imperator
    gloss: An honorific roughly meaning "commander", often given to successful
      Roman generals.
    from: Successful generals were often given the title //[[ imperator ]]//, an
      honorific roughly meaning "commander".
  - term: universal empire
    gloss: Quincy Wright's term for an empire unifying all the contemporary system.
    from: 'Expert on warfare [[ Quincy Wright ]] generalized on what he called
      "universal empire"—empire unifying all the contemporary system:'
  - term: circumscription theory
    gloss: Robert Carneiro's proposition that "the more sharply circumscribed
      area, the more rapidly it will become politically unified".
    from: According to the circumscription theory of [[ Robert Carneiro ]], "the
      more sharply circumscribed area, the more rapidly it will become
      politically unified."[[ ^319 ]]
further:
  - slug: akkadian-empire
    why: The empire Sargon of Akkad established in the 24th century BC, named here
      as an early all-Mesopotamian empire that spread into Anatolia, the
      Levant and Ancient Iran.
  - slug: neo-assyrian-empire
    why: The first empire to recover from the late Bronze Age collapse; by 673 BC
      it had taken the entire Fertile Crescent, including Cyprus and Egypt.
  - slug: macedonia-ancient-kingdom
    why: The article's own example of a territorial empire — a contiguous area
      extending directly outwards from the original frontier.
anchors:
  hook: Many empires endured for centuries, while the age of the ancient
    Egyptian, Chinese and Japanese Empires is counted in millennia. Most
    people throughout history have lived under imperial rule.[[ ^254 ]]
  standfirst: Empires can be traced as far back as the recorded history goes[[
    ^246 ]][[ ^247 ]] and have been the dominant international organization in
    [[ world history | human-history ]] until the 20th century to the least.[[
    ^248 ]][[ ^249 ]][[ ^250 ]][[ ^251 ]] Yet a century ago, most of the world
    was ruled by persons who proudly proclaimed themselves Emperors and were
    proud of their Empires. Of the great powers, only the United States and
    France were republics.[[ ^252 ]]
