  left-out: "Two headings had no prose of their own in the document and are
    carried through with empty bodies: \"Theoretical research\", which goes
    straight into its subsections, and \"Cited sources and further reading\",
    which the document ends on with nothing under it. The records name only
    four image files, so only those four are in the image plan; the article's
    prose also carries about thirty inline maps and gallery pictures (the
    Egyptian, Achaemenid and Seleucid maps in History, the Trajan and Han maps
    in Classical period, the post-classical, gunpowder and modern-period
    galleries, and the timeline chart) which are given as URLs without file
    names. The \"Timeline of empires\" chart is a generated image with a
    clickable map and cannot be reproduced, so that section keeps its
    introduction and its list of notes as they were. The Wikidata claims carry
    no dated events — the only date on them is 2022-10-31, for the article's
    addition to a Wikipedia vital-articles list — so the timeline is drawn
    entirely from the prose. Two apparent slips in the source are transcribed
    as they stand rather than corrected: the 2021 volume is called the
    //Oxford World History of Eire// in the Definition section and the
    //Oxford World History of Empire// in Contemporary usage, and footnote [[
    ^a ]] in the lead is lettered rather than numbered."
